-- ID 1197: Interfund Transactions — Fund-level vs government-wide treatment of transfers
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Westfield County transfers $500,000 from the General Fund to the Capital Projects Fund to finance a new public safety facility. The finance director notes that the transfer appears as an "other financing use" in the General Fund and an "other financing source" in the Capital Projects Fund. However, when preparing the government-wide statements, the transfer disappears entirely from governmental activities. Analyze why this interfund transfer is reported at the fund level but eliminated in the government-wide statements.',
  choices = '["The transfer is eliminated because GASB considers all interfund transfers to be accounting errors that should not appear in any financial statement", "The transfer is reported at the government-wide level as a program revenue of the public safety function because it finances a specific capital project", "The transfer appears at the fund level because fund accounting tracks individual fund resource flows, but it is eliminated in the government-wide statements because both the General Fund and Capital Projects Fund are governmental funds — the transfer is an internal reallocation within governmental activities that does not change total net position, and reporting it would double-count the resource movement", "The transfer is eliminated at the fund level but reported at the government-wide level because GASB requires government-wide statements to show all cash movements between funds"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, interfund transfers between two governmental funds are reported as other financing sources/uses at the fund level because fund accounting requires tracking each fund''s individual inflows and outflows. However, in the government-wide statements, both funds roll up into the single governmental activities column. The $500,000 transfer is merely an internal reallocation — governmental activities neither gained nor lost resources. Including it would overstate both inflows and outflows within governmental activities by $500,000. The elimination preserves the accuracy of the government-wide net position. Choice A is incorrect because transfers are legitimate transactions, not errors — they serve valid budgetary purposes. Choice B is incorrect because internal transfers are not program revenues; they lack an external exchange. Choice D reverses the reporting — transfers appear at the fund level and are eliminated at the government-wide level, not vice versa.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1197;

-- ID 1199: Interfund Transactions — Loan vs transfer comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Maplewood City''s General Fund provides $300,000 to the Enterprise Fund (water utility). The city manager asks whether this should be structured as an interfund loan or an interfund transfer. Under the loan structure, the General Fund reports a receivable and the Enterprise Fund reports a payable; under the transfer structure, the General Fund reports an other financing use and the Enterprise Fund reports an other financing source. Compare the effect of each structure on fund balance and government-wide reporting.',
  choices = '["Both structures produce identical results at both the fund level and government-wide level because GASB treats loans and transfers the same", "The loan preserves the General Fund''s fund balance because it creates a receivable asset rather than reducing fund balance, while the transfer permanently reduces General Fund fund balance by $300,000 — at the government-wide level, the loan appears as an internal balance (receivable/payable) while the transfer between governmental and business-type activities is reported as a transfer and is not eliminated", "The transfer preserves fund balance because transfers are temporary, while loans permanently reduce fund balance because the repayment is not recognized until cash is received", "Neither loans nor transfers affect fund balance because interfund transactions are always eliminated before financial statements are issued"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, an interfund loan creates an asset (due from) in the lending fund and a liability (due to) in the borrowing fund, preserving the General Fund''s fund balance at $300,000. A transfer reduces the General Fund''s fund balance by $300,000 with no expectation of repayment. At the government-wide level, the loan between governmental activities (General Fund) and business-type activities (Enterprise Fund) appears as an internal balance on the statement of net position. The transfer between governmental and business-type activities is not eliminated because it crosses activity categories — it is reported as a transfer on both the governmental activities and business-type activities columns. This cross-activity reporting distinguishes it from transfers within the same activity type, which are eliminated. Choice A is incorrect because the two structures have fundamentally different effects on fund balance and government-wide presentation. Choice C reverses the analysis — transfers permanently reduce fund balance, not loans. Choice D is incorrect because interfund balances and cross-activity transfers are reported, not eliminated.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1199;

-- ID 1203: Interfund Transactions — Elimination vs survival in government-wide statements
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'During fiscal year 2026, Cedar Rapids County engages in three interfund transactions: (1) a $200,000 transfer from the General Fund to the Debt Service Fund, (2) a $150,000 transfer from the General Fund to the Water Enterprise Fund, and (3) a $75,000 reimbursement from the Capital Projects Fund to the General Fund for payroll costs initially paid by the General Fund. When preparing the government-wide financial statements, the finance director must determine which transactions are eliminated and which survive. Analyze the different treatment of these three transactions.',
  choices = '["All three transactions are eliminated because GASB requires removing all interfund activity from government-wide statements to avoid double-counting", "Only Transaction 3 is eliminated because reimbursements are the only interfund transaction type that GASB requires to be removed from government-wide statements", "All three transactions survive in the government-wide statements because GASB requires full transparency of all resource movements", "Transaction 1 (General to Debt Service) is eliminated because both are governmental funds within governmental activities; Transaction 2 (General to Enterprise) survives as an internal activity transfer between governmental and business-type activities; Transaction 3 (reimbursement) is eliminated because reimbursements restore the original fund''s expenditure and reduce the receiving fund''s expenditure, netting to zero"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): GASB Statement No. 34 establishes different government-wide treatments based on transaction type and whether funds are within the same activity category. Transaction 1 is a transfer between two governmental funds — both roll into governmental activities, so the transfer is eliminated to avoid overstating internal resource flows. Transaction 2 crosses activity categories (governmental to business-type), so it survives as a transfer reported in both columns — this accurately depicts the resource flow between the two distinct activity types. Transaction 3 is a reimbursement, not a transfer — it corrects the initial recording by moving the expenditure to the fund that should have borne it. Reimbursements are not reported as transfers; the expenditure is simply reclassified, producing no net effect on governmental activities in the government-wide statements. Choice A is incorrect because cross-activity transfers survive elimination. Choice B is incorrect because same-activity transfers are also eliminated. Choice C is incorrect because elimination of intra-activity transfers is a core requirement of government-wide reporting.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 1203;

-- ID 1205: Interfund Transactions — Internal service fund markup elimination
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Lakewood County operates an internal service fund (ISF) that provides fleet maintenance to all governmental departments. The ISF charges departments $150,000 above its actual costs during fiscal year 2026 to build a vehicle replacement reserve. The finance director is preparing the government-wide reconciliation and must decide how to treat this $150,000 markup. Analyze why the ISF markup must be eliminated and what financial statement distortion would occur if it were not removed.',
  choices = '["The $150,000 markup must be eliminated in the government-wide reconciliation because the ISF''s charges above cost overstate both expenses in the departments (governmental activities) and revenues in the ISF (also rolled into governmental activities) — failure to eliminate would double-count $150,000, inflating both the total expenses and total revenues of governmental activities and distorting the net cost of services reported to citizens", "The markup should not be eliminated because it represents a legitimate revenue source for the ISF and reducing it would understate the ISF''s operating results", "The markup is eliminated only if the ISF serves business-type activities, because markups within governmental activities are permitted under GASB reporting standards", "The ISF markup is automatically eliminated when the ISF is reported as a proprietary fund, so no reconciliation adjustment is necessary"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under GASB Statement No. 34, internal service funds predominantly serve governmental departments and are typically rolled into governmental activities in the government-wide statements. When the ISF charges $150,000 above cost, the governmental departments report $150,000 in excess expenditures and the ISF reports $150,000 in excess revenues. In the government-wide statements, both are within governmental activities, so the markup creates an artificial inflation — $150,000 is counted as both an expense and a revenue within the same reporting column. Eliminating the markup corrects this distortion, ensuring that governmental activities'' net cost of services reflects only the actual cost of fleet maintenance. Without elimination, citizens and analysts would see overstated expenses and overstated revenues that net to zero but misrepresent the true cost structure. Choice B is incorrect because internal markups are not real revenues from the government-wide perspective — they are transfers within the same entity. Choice C is incorrect because the elimination applies precisely because the ISF serves governmental activities. Choice D is incorrect because the reconciliation adjustment is a manual step required during government-wide statement preparation.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1205;

-- ID 3329: Interfund Transactions — Services provided and used vs transfers
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Fairview County''s Water Enterprise Fund pays the General Fund $180,000 for centralized IT services, and the General Fund transfers $200,000 to the Capital Projects Fund for a road improvement project. The county auditor classifies the first transaction as "interfund services provided and used" and the second as an "interfund transfer." Analyze why these two transactions receive fundamentally different accounting treatment, even though both involve resource flows between funds.',
  choices = '["Both transactions should be classified as transfers because all interfund resource flows represent nonreciprocal transfers of resources between funds", "The IT services transaction is reported below the operating line as a transfer because it occurs between funds, while the road transfer is reported as an operating expenditure because it finances a capital asset", "Both transactions are reported as revenues and expenses because GASB requires all interfund activity to be treated as quasi-external regardless of whether an exchange occurs", "The IT services transaction is reported as revenue ($180,000) in the General Fund and as an expense in the Enterprise Fund — like an external transaction — because it involves a genuine exchange of services at approximate market rates; the road transfer is reported as an other financing source/use because it is a nonreciprocal movement of resources with no exchange of goods or services, reflecting a policy decision rather than a market transaction"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): GASB Statement No. 34 distinguishes interfund services provided and used from interfund transfers based on whether a genuine exchange occurs. The IT services transaction qualifies as interfund services provided and used because the General Fund delivers a real service (IT support) to the Enterprise Fund at approximate market value — this mirrors an arm''s-length external transaction, so it is reported as revenue in the providing fund and as an expense in the receiving fund, appearing in the operating section. The road improvement transfer is nonreciprocal — the General Fund moves $200,000 to Capital Projects with no goods or services received in return. It is reported as an other financing use in the General Fund and an other financing source in the Capital Projects Fund, below the operating line. The distinction matters because treating all interfund flows the same would either understate operating revenues (if services were classified as transfers) or overstate them (if transfers were classified as revenues). Choice A is incorrect because interfund services are specifically distinguished from transfers under GASB. Choice B reverses the classification of both transactions. Choice C is incorrect because only genuine exchanges receive revenue/expense treatment.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 3329;

