-- ID 5351: Foreign Currency Transactions — Current rate vs temporal method comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Corp., a U.S. parent, has a subsidiary in Japan whose functional currency is the Japanese yen. Meridian also has a subsidiary in Brazil whose functional currency is the U.S. dollar. Both subsidiaries hold property, plant, and equipment (PP&E) and report revenue in their local currencies. Compare the translation approach applied to each subsidiary and analyze how the choice of method affects the reporting of PP&E, revenue, and the resulting translation or remeasurement adjustment.',
  choices = '["The Japanese subsidiary uses the current rate method (functional currency = yen): PP&E is translated at the current exchange rate, revenue at the weighted-average rate, and the resulting translation adjustment is reported in OCI; the Brazilian subsidiary uses the temporal method (functional currency = USD): PP&E carried at historical cost is remeasured at historical exchange rates, revenue at weighted-average rates, and remeasurement gains/losses flow through the income statement — the method depends entirely on whether the subsidiary''s functional currency is the local currency or the parent''s reporting currency", "Both subsidiaries use the current rate method because all foreign subsidiaries must be translated using end-of-period exchange rates for all items", "Both subsidiaries use the temporal method because U.S. parents always remeasure foreign subsidiaries into U.S. dollars using historical rates", "The Japanese subsidiary uses the temporal method and the Brazilian subsidiary uses the current rate method, because the method is assigned alphabetically by country"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under ASC 830, the translation method depends on the subsidiary''s functional currency. When the functional currency is the local currency (yen for Japan), the current rate method applies: all assets and liabilities are translated at the current exchange rate, revenues and expenses at average rates, and the balancing difference — the cumulative translation adjustment (CTA) — is reported in other comprehensive income (OCI), not in net income. When the functional currency is the reporting currency (USD for Brazil), the temporal method applies: monetary items use the current rate, but nonmonetary items measured at historical cost (like PP&E) use historical exchange rates. Remeasurement gains and losses flow through the income statement because the subsidiary''s books must effectively be recast as if transactions occurred in USD. The distinction reflects economic substance: a yen-functional subsidiary operates independently in yen, so translation adjustments are unrealized and belong in OCI; a USD-functional subsidiary is essentially an extension of the parent, so remeasurement differences are recognized immediately. Choice B is incorrect because the temporal method applies when functional currency is the reporting currency. Choice C is incorrect because the current rate method applies when functional currency is the local currency. Choice D reverses both assignments.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5351;

-- ID 5353: Foreign Currency Transactions — Depreciating currency effect on translated revenue
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nordic Industries, a U.S. parent, owns a subsidiary in Sweden whose functional currency is the Swedish krona (SEK). During fiscal year 2026, the SEK depreciates steadily from $0.10 to $0.08 against the USD. The subsidiary reports SEK 50 million in revenue and holds SEK 40 million in net assets at year-end. Under the current rate method, revenue is translated at the weighted-average rate ($0.09) and net assets at the end-of-period rate ($0.08). Analyze how this rate mismatch creates a cumulative translation adjustment and why ASC 830 directs it to OCI rather than net income.',
  choices = '["No translation adjustment arises because the average rate and end-of-period rate produce the same translated amounts when the currency depreciates steadily", "The translation adjustment flows through net income as a foreign currency gain because all exchange rate effects must be recognized immediately in earnings under ASC 830", "Revenue translated at $0.09 average yields $4.5 million (SEK 50M x $0.09), while net assets translated at $0.08 current rate yield $3.2 million (SEK 40M x $0.08) — the use of different rates for income statement items (average) vs balance sheet items (current) creates a translation adjustment that accumulates in OCI; ASC 830 directs this adjustment to OCI because it is unrealized — the parent has not liquidated its investment in the subsidiary, so the exchange rate effect represents a holding adjustment rather than a realized gain or loss, and routing it through net income would create artificial earnings volatility unrelated to the subsidiary''s operating performance", "The translation adjustment is recorded as a direct reduction to retained earnings because ASC 830 does not permit OCI classification for translation effects"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 830-30, the current rate method translates revenue at the weighted-average rate and balance sheet items at the end-of-period rate. When the foreign currency depreciates during the period, the average rate used for revenue ($0.09) exceeds the current rate used for net assets ($0.08), creating a mismatch. The cumulative translation adjustment (CTA) in OCI absorbs this difference. The rationale for OCI treatment is that the U.S. parent has not disposed of its investment in the Swedish subsidiary — the exchange rate effect is unrealized and may reverse in future periods. Routing it through net income would introduce volatility that does not reflect the subsidiary''s actual operating results in its functional currency. The CTA is only reclassified to net income upon sale or substantially complete liquidation of the foreign entity. Choice A is incorrect because different translation rates for income statement versus balance sheet items inherently create a translation adjustment, even with a steady depreciation. Choice B is incorrect because translation adjustments under the current rate method go to OCI, not net income — only remeasurement gains/losses under the temporal method affect net income. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 830 specifically requires OCI classification for CTAs.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5353;

-- ID 5355: Foreign Currency Transactions — Temporal method inventory measurement basis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Atlas Corp., a U.S. parent, has a subsidiary in Argentina whose functional currency is the U.S. dollar, requiring the temporal method. The subsidiary carries two inventory pools: Pool A at historical cost (ARS 10 million, acquired when the rate was $0.005/ARS) and Pool B written down to net realizable value (ARS 8 million at NRV, current rate $0.003/ARS). Compare how the temporal method translates these two inventory pools and analyze why the measurement basis determines the exchange rate used.',
  choices = '["Both pools use the current exchange rate ($0.003) because all inventory is a current asset and current assets always use the current rate under the temporal method", "Both pools use the historical rate because the temporal method always uses the rate at the date of acquisition for all nonmonetary assets", "Pool A uses the current rate and Pool B uses the historical rate, because NRV items retain their original cost basis for translation purposes", "Pool A (historical cost) is remeasured at the historical rate ($0.005), producing $50,000 (ARS 10M x $0.005), while Pool B (NRV) is remeasured at the current rate ($0.003), producing $24,000 (ARS 8M x $0.003) — the temporal method selects exchange rates based on the measurement attribute, not the asset classification: items measured at historical cost use historical rates to preserve the original dollar equivalent, while items measured at current values (NRV, fair value) use current rates because the measurement itself already reflects current conditions, and using a historical rate would undermine the write-down to current value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The temporal method under ASC 830-10 is grounded in a core principle: the exchange rate should match the measurement attribute. Pool A is measured at historical cost — the rate at acquisition ($0.005) produces the USD equivalent of what was originally spent ($50,000). Using the current rate would distort this historical cost by introducing exchange rate changes that have nothing to do with the asset''s cost basis. Pool B has been written down to net realizable value, a current measurement — the current rate ($0.003) is appropriate because NRV reflects today''s conditions, and the exchange rate should also reflect today''s conditions to produce a meaningful USD equivalent ($24,000). This creates different remeasurement gain/loss patterns: Pool A generates no ongoing remeasurement effect (the historical rate is fixed), while Pool B''s translated value fluctuates with exchange rates. Choice A is incorrect because the temporal method does not base rate selection on asset classification (current vs. noncurrent) but on measurement attribute. Choice B is incorrect because NRV items require the current rate. Choice C reverses the rate assignments.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 5355;

-- ID 5358: Foreign Currency Transactions — Remeasurement vs translation income statement treatment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Pacific Rim Holdings has two foreign subsidiaries: Subsidiary J (functional currency = local currency, translated using the current rate method) and Subsidiary K (functional currency = USD, remeasured using the temporal method). Both subsidiaries experience a 10% depreciation of their local currency against the USD during 2026. Subsidiary J reports a $600,000 translation adjustment and Subsidiary K reports a $450,000 remeasurement loss. Analyze why the translation adjustment goes to OCI while the remeasurement loss goes to the income statement, and explain the effect on earnings volatility.',
  choices = '["Both amounts should go to OCI because all foreign currency effects are unrealized and should not affect net income until the subsidiary is sold", "Both amounts should go to the income statement because GAAP requires immediate recognition of all exchange rate effects in the period they occur", "The translation adjustment ($600,000) goes to OCI because Subsidiary J operates independently in its local currency — the CTA represents an unrealized holding effect on the parent''s net investment that may reverse, and recognizing it in income would create volatility unrelated to J''s operating performance; the remeasurement loss ($450,000) goes to the income statement because Subsidiary K''s functional currency is the USD, meaning its foreign-currency-denominated transactions create real economic exposures that are settled in USD terms — these gains and losses reflect actual economic effects on the parent''s dollar-denominated results, justifying immediate income recognition", "The classification depends solely on the amount — adjustments over $500,000 go to OCI and those under $500,000 go to the income statement"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 830 establishes different income statement treatments based on the economic relationship between the subsidiary and the parent. When the functional currency is the local currency (Subsidiary J), the subsidiary''s operations are self-contained in that currency. Translation to USD is a reporting convenience, and the resulting adjustment is unrealized — it represents the effect of exchange rate changes on the parent''s net investment position. OCI treatment prevents artificial earnings volatility from these unrealized holding effects. When the functional currency is the USD (Subsidiary K), the subsidiary essentially operates in dollars through a foreign currency overlay. Remeasurement gains and losses reflect real economic exposures because the subsidiary''s performance is measured in USD. A $450,000 remeasurement loss represents an actual reduction in the USD value of the subsidiary''s net monetary position, warranting immediate recognition. This distinction directly affects earnings volatility: Subsidiary K''s remeasurement losses create period-to-period income fluctuations, while Subsidiary J''s translation adjustments bypass income entirely until disposal. Choice A is incorrect because remeasurement losses under the temporal method are considered realized for reporting purposes. Choice B is incorrect because translation adjustments under the current rate method specifically go to OCI. Choice D is incorrect because the classification depends on economic substance (functional currency determination), not dollar thresholds.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5358;

-- ID 5359: Foreign Currency Transactions — Transaction gain/loss vs intercompany translation adjustment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Orion Inc., a U.S. company, has a CAD 1,000,000 receivable from a Canadian customer recorded when the exchange rate was $0.75/CAD. At year-end, the rate is $0.72/CAD. Separately, Orion''s wholly owned Canadian subsidiary (functional currency = CAD) has an intercompany receivable from Orion of CAD 1,000,000 that is considered part of Orion''s long-term net investment in the subsidiary. Analyze how the same $30,000 exchange rate effect is treated differently for the third-party receivable versus the intercompany receivable, and explain the rationale under ASC 830.',
  choices = '["The third-party receivable produces a $30,000 transaction loss recognized in net income under ASC 830-20 because it is a monetary item that will be settled in CAD, creating a realized economic exposure; the intercompany receivable also produces a $30,000 exchange rate effect, but under ASC 830-20-35-3 it is reported in the cumulative translation adjustment in OCI because the receivable is of a long-term investment nature with settlement neither planned nor anticipated — the exchange rate effect is treated as part of the parent''s net investment in the subsidiary rather than a standalone transaction gain or loss", "Both receivables produce a $30,000 foreign currency transaction loss reported in net income because all exchange rate effects on receivables are treated identically under ASC 830", "The third-party receivable has no exchange rate effect because it was recorded at the historical rate, while the intercompany receivable produces a $30,000 gain because intercompany balances are always favorable", "Both produce a $30,000 gain rather than a loss because the CAD depreciation benefits the U.S. parent by reducing the USD equivalent of its CAD-denominated obligations"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): ASC 830 distinguishes between foreign currency transaction gains/losses and translation adjustments based on the nature and expected settlement of the monetary item. The third-party CAD receivable will be collected and converted to USD, so the $30,000 decline ($750,000 to $720,000) is a realized transaction loss reported in net income — Orion will receive fewer dollars when the receivable is collected. The intercompany receivable is classified as part of the net investment in the Canadian subsidiary under ASC 830-20-35-3 because settlement is neither planned nor anticipated in the foreseeable future. The same $30,000 exchange rate effect is reported in OCI as part of the cumulative translation adjustment because it functions economically like an equity investment in the subsidiary rather than a trade receivable awaiting collection. This distinction prevents long-term intercompany financing arrangements from creating income statement volatility that does not reflect actual cash flow expectations. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 830 specifically carves out long-term investment-nature intercompany balances from income statement recognition. Choice C is incorrect because both receivables are affected by the rate change — foreign-currency-denominated monetary items are remeasured at each reporting date. Choice D is incorrect because the CAD depreciation creates a loss, not a gain, for a holder of CAD-denominated receivables.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5359;

