-- ID 5370: Intangible Assets Advanced — Goodwill impairment one-step vs two-step comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Keystone Industries has a reporting unit with a carrying amount of $15 million, including $4 million of goodwill. The reporting unit''s fair value is determined to be $12.5 million based on a discounted cash flow analysis. Under the current simplified goodwill impairment test (ASU 2017-04), the impairment loss would be $2.5 million. Compare this result to what would have occurred under the former two-step approach, and analyze why FASB simplified the test.',
  choices = '["Under both approaches, the impairment loss is $4 million because any impairment requires a full write-off of goodwill regardless of the fair value shortfall", "The one-step approach always produces a larger impairment loss than the two-step approach because it ignores the fair value of identifiable assets", "Under the current one-step approach, the impairment loss equals the excess of carrying amount over fair value, capped at goodwill: $15M − $12.5M = $2.5M (which is less than the $4M goodwill, so the full $2.5M is recognized); under the former two-step approach, step 2 required a hypothetical purchase price allocation of the $12.5M fair value to all identifiable net assets to determine implied goodwill — if identifiable net assets were valued at $11.5M, implied goodwill would be $1M, producing only a $3M impairment ($4M − $1M); FASB eliminated step 2 because the hypothetical PPA was costly, time-consuming, and often produced different results than the simplified approach without providing decision-useful information proportional to the cost", "Under the current approach, no impairment is recognized because the fair value exceeds the goodwill balance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASU 2017-04 (codified in ASC 350-20-35), the current one-step test compares the reporting unit''s fair value ($12.5M) to its carrying amount ($15M). The impairment loss is the difference ($2.5M), capped at the goodwill carrying amount ($4M). Under the former two-step approach (pre-ASU 2017-04), step 1 identified impairment, but step 2 required performing a hypothetical purchase price allocation — assigning the $12.5M fair value across all identifiable assets and liabilities as if they were acquired in a business combination. Only the residual was implied goodwill, and impairment was the excess of recorded goodwill over implied goodwill. This second step could produce a smaller impairment if identifiable net assets had appreciated. FASB eliminated step 2 because it imposed significant cost and complexity, particularly the need for appraisals of all identifiable assets, while the incremental information was not proportionate to the effort. Choice A is incorrect because impairment is measured as the shortfall, not a full write-off. Choice B is not universally true — the one-step approach can produce either larger or smaller impairment depending on the fair values of identifiable assets. Choice D is incorrect because the fair value ($12.5M) is less than the carrying amount ($15M), triggering impairment.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5370;

-- ID 5373: Intangible Assets Advanced — Indefinite-lived vs finite-lived impairment comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Atlas Corp. acquired two intangible assets in a business combination: a trade name classified as indefinite-lived (carrying value $5 million) and a customer list classified as finite-lived with a 10-year useful life (carrying value $3 million). Three years after acquisition, a shift in consumer preferences reduces the trade name''s fair value to $3.5 million. Analyze the impairment treatment of the trade name, compare it to the ongoing accounting for the customer list, and explain why indefinite-lived intangibles are not amortized but are subject to annual impairment testing.',
  choices = '["Both assets are amortized over 10 years and tested for impairment only when triggering events occur — the indefinite-lived classification has no effect on accounting treatment", "The trade name is not amortized because its useful life is indefinite — there is no foreseeable limit on the period over which it will generate cash flows — but it must be tested for impairment annually (or when triggering events occur); the $1.5M impairment ($5M − $3.5M) is recognized immediately in earnings; the customer list is amortized ($300,000/year) because its cash flows are expected to diminish over 10 years, and its carrying value ($3M − $900K amortization = $2.1M at year 3) would be tested for impairment only if a triggering event occurs; the distinction reflects that amortization systematically allocates cost over a finite benefit period, while indefinite-lived assets require impairment testing as the sole mechanism to ensure the carrying value does not exceed fair value", "The trade name is written off entirely ($5 million) because any impairment of an indefinite-lived intangible requires full write-off, while the customer list continues to be amortized with no impairment test", "Both assets are impaired by $1.5 million because impairment losses must be equal across all intangible assets acquired in the same business combination"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 350-30, indefinite-lived intangible assets are not amortized because there is no foreseeable limit on the cash flows they are expected to generate. However, this means the carrying value is never systematically reduced, creating a risk of overstatement. Annual impairment testing (comparing fair value to carrying amount) serves as the control mechanism. Atlas''s trade name impairment is $1.5 million ($5M carrying value − $3.5M fair value), recognized immediately in the income statement. The customer list, by contrast, is amortized over its 10-year useful life because the economic benefits are expected to diminish predictably. After 3 years, accumulated amortization is $900,000 ($3M / 10 years x 3), leaving a carrying value of $2.1 million. Finite-lived intangibles are tested for impairment only when events or circumstances indicate the carrying amount may not be recoverable (ASC 360 triggering event model), not annually. Choice A is incorrect because indefinite-lived intangibles are explicitly not amortized under ASC 350. Choice C is incorrect because impairment is limited to the excess of carrying value over fair value, not a full write-off. Choice D is incorrect because impairment is assessed individually for each asset based on its own fair value.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5373;

-- ID 5374: Intangible Assets Advanced — IPR&D lifecycle accounting
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Vanguard Biotech acquires PharmaCo in a business combination and identifies $8 million of in-process research and development (IPR&D) related to a drug candidate in Phase II clinical trials. The IPR&D is recognized at fair value as an indefinite-lived intangible asset. Two years later, the drug receives FDA approval and is expected to generate revenue over 15 years. Analyze the accounting treatment at each stage: acquisition, post-acquisition while in process, project completion, and how the treatment would differ if the project were abandoned instead of completed.',
  choices = '["IPR&D is expensed immediately at acquisition because research costs cannot be capitalized under GAAP, and no further accounting is needed regardless of whether the project succeeds or fails", "IPR&D is amortized from the acquisition date over its expected development period, and no adjustment is made upon completion or abandonment", "IPR&D is recorded as goodwill at acquisition because its future benefits are uncertain, and it is reclassified as a separate intangible only upon project completion", "At acquisition, IPR&D is recognized at $8M fair value under ASC 805 as an indefinite-lived intangible asset — it is not amortized while in development because the useful life is considered indefinite until the project''s outcome is known; it is tested annually for impairment; upon FDA approval, the asset is reclassified as a finite-lived intangible and amortized over its 15-year useful life; if the project were abandoned instead, the entire carrying amount would be written off as an impairment loss — the three-stage treatment reflects the evolving certainty of future cash flows: uncertain during development, determinable upon completion, and zero upon abandonment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): ASC 805-20-30 requires IPR&D acquired in a business combination to be recognized at fair value as a separate intangible asset, not expensed. This differs from internally generated R&D, which is generally expensed as incurred under ASC 730. The acquired IPR&D is classified as indefinite-lived during development because the useful life cannot be determined until the project succeeds or fails. Annual impairment testing under ASC 350-30 ensures the carrying value remains supportable. Upon completion (FDA approval), the asset transitions to a finite-lived classification and begins amortization over its expected revenue-generating period (15 years). If abandoned, the asset has no future cash flows and is written off entirely. This three-stage lifecycle reflects the matching principle: costs are recognized against the revenues they generate, and the timing of recognition adjusts as uncertainty resolves. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 805 specifically requires capitalization of acquired IPR&D, unlike internally developed R&D. Choice B is incorrect because IPR&D is not amortized during development — it is indefinite-lived until completion. Choice C is incorrect because IPR&D is a separately identifiable intangible, not part of goodwill.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 5374;

-- ID 5382: Intangible Assets Advanced — Defensive intangible asset analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Trident Industries acquires a competitor''s consumer brand for $2 million in an asset acquisition. Trident has no intention of marketing products under this brand — the sole purpose is to prevent competitors from using it. The controller questions whether an asset with no revenue-generating potential should be recognized on the balance sheet. Analyze why this defensive intangible asset is recognized at fair value under ASC 805 despite having no direct revenue potential, and explain the typical useful life and amortization pattern.',
  choices = '["The brand should not be recognized because GAAP only permits recognition of intangible assets that directly generate revenue — a defensive asset has no future economic benefit and must be expensed immediately", "Defensive intangible assets are classified as goodwill because they cannot be separately identified or valued apart from the overall business synergies of the acquisition", "The brand is recognized at $2M fair value because ASC 805 requires recognition of all identifiable intangible assets regardless of the acquirer''s intended use — the asset''s value lies in the competitive benefit of preventing rivals from using the brand, which is a measurable economic benefit even though it produces no direct revenue; the useful life is typically short because the acquirer has no plans to maintain or develop the brand, so it is amortized over the period of expected competitive benefit (often the shorter of the legal protection period or the time until the brand loses market relevance), with a pattern reflecting the declining defensive value", "The brand is recognized at $2M but classified as indefinite-lived because it can be legally protected forever through trademark renewal, and no amortization is required"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 805-20-25 requires acquirers to recognize all identifiable intangible assets at fair value, regardless of whether the acquirer intends to use them actively. A defensive intangible asset provides economic benefit by eliminating a competitive threat — the $2 million reflects the present value of the competitive advantage gained by keeping the brand out of rivals'' hands. This benefit is real and measurable, even though it generates no direct revenue stream. The useful life is typically finite and relatively short because the acquirer will not invest in maintaining the brand''s market presence. Amortization reflects the period over which the defensive benefit exists — as the brand loses relevance in the marketplace (due to lack of marketing support), its competitive threat diminishes. The asset is typically amortized on a straight-line or accelerated basis over this period. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 805 does not limit recognition to revenue-generating assets — competitive protection is a legitimate economic benefit. Choice B is incorrect because the brand meets the contractual-legal criterion for separate identification (it is a registered trademark). Choice D is incorrect because the acquirer''s decision not to maintain the brand typically results in a finite useful life, even if the trademark could theoretically be renewed.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5382;

