-- ID 5330: Business Combinations — Acquisition cost expensing analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cascade Holdings acquires Silverline Corp. for $50 million and incurs $2 million in investment banking fees and legal costs related to the acquisition. Under ASC 805, these acquisition-related costs must be expensed as incurred. The CFO of Cascade notes that under the former SFAS 141 approach, these costs would have been capitalized as part of the acquisition cost and included in goodwill. Analyze the financial statement effects of expensing versus capitalizing these costs in the acquisition year and in subsequent periods.',
  choices = '["Expensing and capitalizing produce identical total income over time because the $2M is recognized as an expense either way — the only difference is labeling on the balance sheet", "Capitalizing is always preferable because it matches the cost with the future benefits of the acquired business and produces higher earnings in the acquisition year", "Under ASC 805, the $2M is recognized as a reduction to the purchase price, decreasing the total consideration to $48M and reducing goodwill accordingly", "Expensing under ASC 805 reduces acquisition-year income by $2M immediately while leaving goodwill at $50M; capitalizing under the former approach would have increased goodwill by $2M (to $52M) with no immediate income statement impact — however, the capitalized costs would have been subject to goodwill impairment testing in subsequent periods; the expensing approach produces lower acquisition-year income but higher post-acquisition goodwill carrying values are avoided, reducing the likelihood and magnitude of future impairment charges; FASB chose expensing because acquisition costs do not represent assets of the acquired entity — they are costs of executing the transaction, analogous to transaction costs in other contexts"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): ASC 805-10-25-23 requires acquisition-related costs to be expensed as incurred in the periods the costs are recognized. This creates an immediate $2 million hit to acquisition-year income that would not have occurred under the former capitalization approach. Under SFAS 141, these costs would have been part of the acquisition cost, increasing goodwill from $50M to $52M. The income statement would have been unaffected in the acquisition year, but the additional $2M of goodwill would have been subject to annual impairment testing in perpetuity, potentially creating impairment charges in future periods. FASB''s rationale for the change was conceptual: acquisition costs represent the cost of executing the transaction, not an asset of the acquired entity. They do not generate future economic benefits independent of the business acquired, so capitalizing them would overstate the fair value of net assets obtained. Choice A is incorrect because the total income effects differ — under capitalization, the $2M would only reduce income if and when goodwill is impaired, which might never occur. Choice B reflects a preference for the old approach but ignores FASB''s conceptual rationale. Choice C is incorrect because acquisition costs are not a reduction of purchase price — they are period expenses separate from the consideration transferred.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 5330;

-- ID 5335: Business Combinations — Step acquisition remeasurement analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Reed Co. holds a 30% equity-method investment in Thorn Inc. with a carrying amount of $9 million. Reed acquires the remaining 70% of Thorn for $28 million in cash when Thorn''s total fair value is $40 million. On the acquisition date, Reed''s 30% interest has a fair value of $12 million ($40M x 30%). Analyze why ASC 805 requires Reed to remeasure its previously held 30% interest at fair value, how the resulting gain is calculated and recognized, and the effect on the total goodwill calculation.',
  choices = '["ASC 805-10-25-10 requires remeasuring the previously held 30% interest at acquisition-date fair value ($12M), producing a $3M gain ($12M − $9M carrying amount) recognized in earnings — this gain reflects the difference between the investment''s historical equity-method basis and its current fair value; total consideration is then $40M ($28M cash + $12M remeasured interest), and goodwill equals total consideration minus the fair value of Thorn''s identifiable net assets; the remeasurement is required because the step acquisition transforms Reed''s relationship from investor to parent, and GAAP treats this as a single acquisition at fair value rather than blending two transactions at different measurement dates", "Reed continues to carry the 30% interest at its $9M equity-method basis and calculates goodwill based only on the $28M cash payment for the 70% interest, because the pre-existing investment is not affected by the new acquisition", "Reed recognizes a $3M loss because the equity-method carrying amount exceeds the fair value of the 30% interest at the acquisition date", "No remeasurement occurs because ASC 805 only applies to the newly acquired 70% interest, and the 30% interest retains its equity-method accounting permanently"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): ASC 805-10-25-10 requires that when control is obtained through a step acquisition, the acquirer remeasures its previously held equity interest at acquisition-date fair value and recognizes any resulting gain or loss in earnings. Reed''s 30% interest has a carrying amount of $9 million under the equity method and a fair value of $12 million ($40M total fair value x 30%), producing a $3 million gain recognized in the income statement. The total consideration for goodwill purposes is $40 million: $28 million in cash for the 70% plus $12 million for the remeasured 30% interest. Goodwill is then $40 million minus the fair value of Thorn''s identifiable net assets. The conceptual basis for remeasurement is that the acquisition fundamentally changes Reed''s relationship with Thorn — from an investor exercising significant influence to a parent exercising control. GAAP treats the business combination as a single transaction measured at fair value on the acquisition date, not as a blending of the original investment basis with the new purchase price. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 805 explicitly requires remeasurement of all previously held interests when control is achieved. Choice C reverses the gain calculation — fair value ($12M) exceeds carrying amount ($9M), producing a gain, not a loss. Choice D is incorrect because the step acquisition provisions of ASC 805 apply to the entire interest, including the previously held portion.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5335;

COMMIT;
